  "client_view_text": " A write-ahead log is a file or set of files that contain every operation that is committed to the database, in the order they are applied. This includes not just the newer transactions but all history. This is contrast to a physical journal, which contains only the latest changes. When the database system crashes, it is possible to revert the state of the database to its previous state by replaying the undo information in the history log. How does this restore the database to a consistent state? A read-only database state and a point-in-time recovery are also supported by the log.\n\n## Step 1: Understanding the concept of a write-ahead log\nA write-ahead log is a mechanism used in database systems to ensure data consistency and integrity by recording all committed operations in a specific order. This includes not just recent transactions but the entire history of the database. It acts as a record of every update made to the database, providing a snapshot of the system's state at any point in time.\n\n## Step 2: Crash recovery process with a write-ahead log\nWhen a database system crashes, the write-ahead log is used to recover the database to a consistent state. The process involves reapplying the transactions recorded in the log to the database since the last successful checkpoint. This is done"
}